The Psychoanalyst's Perspective (Episode 32): The Cultural Land of Iran 2

Greetings to my dear fellow compatriots and all Persian speakers.

In the thirty-second episode of 'The Psychoanalyst's Perspective', I continue the discussion concerning the book *Another Look at the Cultural Land of Iran*. In the previous episode, I outlined its general themes. Now I wish to discuss more specifically the first essay of this book ('Iran, the Cultural Land of Us Iranians'). First, I must point out a few preliminary remarks—much like Hafez, who takes preventative care when praising and celebrating the Magian Elder (*Pir-e Moghan*) and answers in advance the questions that might arise regarding the Magian Elder. The cultural land of us Iranians has no connection whatsoever with chauvinism, cultural supremacism, the violence of a system of cultural superiority over others, or considering the Persian language in any way superior to other languages. Under no circumstances do I move in that direction. Rather, it is a broader, more historical, and anthropological perspective, engaging in an archaeology of knowledge. In my view, it is a perspective that may perhaps gather all fellow compatriots around itself, precisely because it is altogether non-political and entirely anthropological. Gradually, these data, pieces of information, and elements deepen our understanding of the cultural land and enable us to look at it impartially—whether through the expansion of archaeology, through discovering texts to which we previously had no access, through a more precise understanding of the history of other cultures, or through recognising and recalling the influence of Persian-speaking culture within other cultures. In other words, before we look upon the cultural land of Iran like a lover gazing upon the beloved, let us examine it as far as possible through an anthropological lens, striving for a perspective that is realistic, grounded in records and documents, and situated firmly within the discipline of anthropology.

I have offered this introduction so that prejudices may be cleared away to some extent. Whoever speaks of the cultural land of Iran does not mean to erroneously invoke a two-thousand-five-hundred-year continuous imperial monarchy—which did not exist as such and belongs to imagination and fantasy—nor to claim that the Persian language is sweeter than all other languages, or that Iranian culture is a primary or superior culture. Priority and posteriority have no place among cultures. Cultures have emerged gradually over the course of modern human existence. Over one hundred thousand to one hundred and fifty thousand years of cultural life, human beings created various languages for themselves and invented diverse traditions that answered the needs of society and life in their particular time and space; likewise, through curiosity, they enriched their presence in existence, and by attaining forms of knowledge that allowed their lives to transcend earlier limits—such as fire, the wheel, the compass, and the circle—they expanded culture. When we look at the thirteen billion human beings who have lived across the formation of cultures, and the seven billion human beings alive today, what exists are the achievements that have been gathered within the framework of cultures. It is these cultures that have collected human achievements within their vessels: whether tools of production, modes of production, ways of speaking, marriage traditions, relationships with children, or diverse worldviews and religions—all are the achievements of these thirteen billion people, and all are valuable because they represent the endeavour of one hundred and fifty thousand years of movement by our forebears.

As far as can be determined, human beings have invented roughly one hundred and forty thousand languages thus far, of which six thousand remain alive today. A living language is one spoken by more than three thousand people; if the number of speakers falls below that threshold, they cannot sustain the language and it dies out. One of these six thousand languages is Persian. The Persian language is used within Iranian culture, which is itself a constellation of cultures. People living in Iran today have Persian either as their mother tongue or as their common language. Our Kurdish compatriots naturally have Kurdish as their mother tongue; yet when they enter the wider public sphere, or if I am a guest in their home, they speak Persian—speaking it most beautifully and pleasantly, with a very ancient cadence. We are able to understand one another because we live within a single nation-state, we communicate with one another, we share family ties, marriages have taken place, and children have been born whose fathers and mothers had different mother tongues. In my view, this multiculturalism can bring nothing but richness to the people of a country, allowing cultural exchange to reach into the very homes themselves. I have made this observation because, in the discussions to follow regarding the cultural land of Iran, we are casting a spotlight from a psychoanalyst's perspective. Naturally, some prejudice might arise if you do not follow my words as closely as required. My request is that you set prejudice aside a little. In the conferences, workshops, and lectures I have delivered, I have always offered these reminders. Of course, there have always been a few recurring criticisms: for example, the view that regardless of where we stand, if we take systems of production as the foundation and say that the productive system is the base while culture is the superstructure, then culture has little intrinsic value in itself, and instead the problems of modes of production and class struggle must be resolved first. That perspective is worthy of respect; yet it should not prevent us from speaking about our own cultural land. Another criticism concerns ethnocentrism or ultranationalism—indulging in illusions about two thousand five hundred years of monarchy which, if we are realistic, did not exist in that unbroken form. There are other criticisms as well. There are friends who conflate the discussion of the cultural land of Iran with the Islamic Republic of Iran under current conditions, and if they harbour grievances against the regime of the Islamic Republic, they transfer that resentment—perhaps inadvertently—onto the cultural land of Iran, which is hardly accurate. Separating these two from one another is essential, because across the at least seven thousand years of which we have knowledge—even though our information becomes more limited the further back we go into the depths of time—we nevertheless know concrete things from Shahr-e Sokhteh (the Burnt City) onwards, enabling us to gradually conceive and trace a historical continuity for ourselves. This culture and this language have lived on through Pahlavi, Middle Persian, and down to the Dari Persian of today. In my judgement, any language that borrows words from other languages demonstrates the intelligence of that language and the resourcefulness of the people who speak it. We know that the Chinese language likewise has roots going back at least six thousand years, and it is true that, like Persian, it has endured through history; yet in Persian we see that many words remain the very same as they were in Pahlavi. Between twenty and thirty per cent of its vocabulary is Arabic, primarily connected to philosophy, science, and religious subjects. Another ten per cent consists of non-Persian and non-Arabic loanwords, such as Turkish, English, and Russian. Thus, this language and this culture form a living culture which, looked at another way, has travelled from the distant past, encountered manifold historical events, taught other cultures and learnt from them, lent words and borrowed words. Regarding horsemanship, where we lacked a specific term, we borrowed it from Turkish. When tea arrived in Iran, the word *samovar* came along with it, which we borrowed from Russian and continue to use to this day. This demonstrates the intelligence of this language. I have raised these points by way of prevention so that I may speak somewhat more freely about the psychoanalyst's perspective on the cultural land of Iran.

I would like to read further from the introduction to this book, because it appears to convey very concisely what I have in mind and ensures that I have done justice to the subject. Our cultural land is at once the earth, the mountain, the sky, and the sea; it is the society and the people who have departed and those who remain; and it is the entirety of our oral and written culture. This definitional domain that I endeavour to present here marks the boundaries of this cultural land, and there is great scope for us to know this cultural land far better. Today, given the virtual sphere—radio stations, television, the internet, and all the virtual social networks—we can say that our cultural land is breathing, and this culture does not wait for a poem by Hafez to be printed in a book or a newspaper. Day and night, across the entirety of this virtual space, that poem circulates; an apt image and a beautiful melody circulate day and night across these social networks, and the vitality of this culture today lies in its virtual presence. Consequently, no barrier, no government, and no group—even with all state resources and capital investment at its disposal—can possibly halt it. It is therefore the great fortune of our generation that we can pass this ancient and beautiful banner from hand to hand across the entire domain of this cultural land using the virtual sphere. When we examine it, it comprises both the earth, the sky, the mountain, and the sea, and the society and the people who have departed and those who remain. The Indigenous peoples of Australia, who have suffered a deeply tragic historical fate, have a very different way of slicing reality and existence. We are horizon-oriented: if we wish to draw a picture, we draw it along the horizon. They hold a different view. For them, life continues underground, and their fathers, mothers, and ancestors who are buried in the earth speak with them. They place their ears to the ground and seek counsel from their ancestors, because they believe their ancestors continue their lives beneath the earth. In another sense, we too can say that the cultural land of Iran lies beneath the ground as well—both in its symbolic or virtual form (for instance, our ancestors who are buried here) and in its real, material form (for instance, the village and site of Pasargadae, which everyone knows can yield immense knowledge, information, and data upon excavation). We saw what resistance arose and how deeply people cared when a dam was planned that would have flooded an ancient settlement buried underground—because one day we may have the opportunity to excavate beneath the surface and bring more of our past to light. For this reason, it is at once the earth, the mountain, the sky, and the sea, as well as the society and the people who have departed and those who remain. Those who departed transmitted the language to their children, and it matured, transformed, was cultivated, blended with other languages, and gradually moved forward, both orally and in writing. Fortunately, for nearly a hundred years now, scholars, scientists, and lovers of Iran have turned to oral culture to collect and record it in writing as far as possible. Deeply dedicated figures such as Anjavi Shirazi began this work, and the late Ahmad Shamlou continued it. On the other hand, the very first task undertaken by Allameh Dehkhoda was the collection of proverbs, followed by his monumental dictionary (*Loghatnameh*), a work that has continued down to the present day. We see that the people who have departed—what constituted their history, what constituted their very breath—has remained in this land. Every movement of theirs has endured, and this is the cultural land.

Across this entire region, we are among the original inhabitants, the eldest of all, and our place is that of the Magian Elder (*Pir-e Moghan*). There were others too, such as the Neanderthals, who unfortunately perished. Our ancestors showed them no mercy, and they themselves, lacking the necessary developments, could not withstand the pressure. Until ten years ago, it was assumed that they had migrated further north across Asia; today, however, we know that they also passed through Iran. Some researchers have even suggested that the cave-dwelling *Divs* (daemons) in the *Shahnameh* represent the image preserved in the collective memory of the Neanderthals. I mention this with due caution, though published literature on this hypothesis exists and perhaps one day we shall examine it.

In my view, in defining this cultural land, it must be said that all the prestige and value of the cultural name of Iran lies embedded within the volume of this cultural land—within a volume comprising:

— All diverse religious beliefs, such as Shia, Sunni, Ismaili, Zoroastrian, Christian, Jewish, Baha'i, denominations such as Assyrian and Armenian Christians who maintain their distinct traditions, even our Sikh compatriots in Baluchestan or Buddhists who are gradually present in certain cities, and indeed even any two individuals who gather together and profess a belief—a belief which, within the framework of social laws, may be considered inviolable and sacred;

— All oral and written cultures;

— All rock inscriptions, manuscripts, and ancient monuments;

— All historical infrastructures, bridges, ports, and *qanats* (underground aqueducts);

— All extinct languages and living languages and dialects, such as Kurdish, Turkish, Baluchi, Luri, and Arabic;

— All productive, industrial, and artistic methods and fine crafts;

— All traditions, dances, games, festivals, and especially the celebration of Nowruz;

— All natural and mineral resources, the richness of nature, wildlife, plants, trees, and rivers.

All the prestige and value of the cultural name of Iran lies within the volume of this cultural land.

When news reports and short video clips arrive from the flood-stricken people of dear Iran, it is deeply painful. I feel this pain within my own being, and surely other compatriots feel the same. It is heart-wrenching when a family sees that nothing—truly nothing—remains of their home. All of their memories (weddings, the passing of loved ones, births, and so on) were in that house, and now nothing remains of it save washed-out earth. I grieve profoundly with each and every one of these families, and I reflect that humanity surely possesses solutions to prevent natural disasters from turning into such catastrophes. From a technical standpoint, solutions fortunately exist; one need only gain mastery over them and put them to use so that natural hazards cannot sweep everything away, just as this recent flood has acted like a broom, particularly in Lorestan where it has swept everything before it. I hope we hear good news as soon as possible—that we hear these rains have ceased and the dangers have passed, and furthermore, that the combined resources of the public, the state, and various civic associations have managed to provide at least a minimum standard of living for these dear people. Fortunately, the weather is not excessively cold, except in mountainous regions. With this heartfelt wish, I entrust my compatriots to God.

