The Psychoanalyst's Gaze (Manageability) (Part Ten)

Greetings and salutations, with wishes for good and beautiful days for all my compatriots.

In today's programme of *The Psychoanalyst's Gaze*, I intend to speak about management and, more specifically, manageability. The problem with theories of management is mainly that, according to them, a manager must direct collective work, lead it, and bring it from one point to another—whether in production, in everyday life, or in major social movements. A manager usually has two primary duties. One is the duty of administering; the other is the duty of guiding and showing the way. That is to say, a manager may merely administer—meaning that what exists today remains just like yesterday, in effect treading water, and administering that stagnation well. They may administer well the group and individuals placed under their authority, or even the city, society, or country entrusted to them; relationships may continue in an orderly fashion, yet without any clear long-term strategy, without wishing to guide from one point to another or to show a way forward. For this reason, one kind of manager is an administrator, whereas another kind is a guide who points out the path. There have been periods in human history where society marked time, and at times even regressed: for example, the European Middle Ages, or Iran a century after the emergence of the Safavids, when the seminaries of the Shi'ite clerics took shape and absorbed as much of the education of Iranian society as they could, until the late nineteenth century, when forms of education arrived from the West and gradually carved out a place for themselves. During that interval, Iranian society was not strategically led; it was merely administered, in a decentralised, fief-like manner by khans, clerics, and to some extent the bazaar merchants—and, in a far more secondary way, perhaps with some involvement of Sufi lodge (*khanaqah*) and dervish networks. On the whole, however, over those two hundred and fifty years, one may say that Iranian society was mostly administered, and administered very poorly. It was here that we suffered the greatest damage in our cultural history. Perhaps in the future I shall dissect this period, which was an exceedingly ominous and defiling era for Iranian culture. Investigate and see: its political product was Shah Sultan Husayn; its social, and indeed cultural and economic, product was the destruction of all earlier foundations—we see its effect even in architecture, compared with what was created at the beginning of Shah Abbas's reign, such as the Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque. Sadly and unfortunately, when history is taught, it is presented as though this were the fault of the Qajar era; in my view, however, even the Qajar era itself was under the sway of that dominant atmosphere produced by the seminaries of the Shi'ite clerics. During that period, society was merely administered: there was no strategy at all, and the question of the future, of building the future and advancing society, was never raised in any way—until, fortunately, saving voices were heard from the West and a movement began that culminated in the Constitutional Revolution.

A manager must both administer society and show the way—must have a strategy. In faculties and universities, economic management and military management are most often taught first, followed by political management, and finally other branches of management, because society has greater need of the first two. In the teaching of management, various theories are put forward; yet in most of them, practitioners do not confront the question of what kind of culture we are facing. As an anthropologist, I try to begin from below, from the people—that is, to pay attention to the manageability of a culture. If a management model or meta-pattern succeeds and takes effect in one society, will it also yield results in another? Is the culture of this society receptive to manageability or not? Until we understand the manageability of a people's culture, we cannot adapt our management models to that manageability. For this reason, we must take a deeper look at the manageability of the Iranian people, to see how this manageability has evolved and what kind of manageability Iranians possess today. For instance, from the time of Ghazan Khan (seventh century AH) until six hundred years later (1342 SH / 1963), when land reform was carried out, Iranian agriculture rested upon a management system known as the landlord-and-peasant (*arbab-o-ra'iyat*) relationship. We never had feudalism; rather, we had the *arbab-o-ra'iyat* system. Fortunately, extensive studies have been conducted on the landlord-peasant relationship. This relationship was based on *nasaq* (customary sharecropping rights), and the yield produced was divided into five parts according to the following factors:

Human labour;

Animal power;

Seed;

Land;

Water.

For example, if a landlord owned the land, had also brought the water—having repaired the *qanat* and constructed the channel (thus owning the water)—had supplied the draught animal, and had provided the seed, then one-fifth of the harvest went to the labour force (the peasant, or *ra'iyat*). If the water was public, the one-fifth share for water was spent on maintaining the water supply and *qanat*. If the draught animal belonged to the peasant, his share became two-fifths. If the peasant also provided the seed himself, his share became three-fifths. The tradition of *nasaq* remained in force until 1342 (1963). When this tradition was broken all at once (within the span of six years) and it was decided to transfer land ownership to the peasants, the productive foundation of roughly fifty per cent of society changed. Under this new system of management, the peasant had to manage his own land and his own farming. At the very same time that the decision was taken to alter agricultural management, formal schooling for peasants' children also began. See what sudden violence occurred: the result was the migration of approximately one million eight hundred thousand people, out of a population of thirty-seven million at that time, to the major cities. When we break a society's means of production, the readiness of the people to return to the tradition of their forebears increases. Naturally, when these landless villagers (*khosh-neshin*) or even a number of peasants, unable to manage what they possessed, moved to the outskirts of large cities, they became marginalised urban dwellers who could turn into cheap foot-soldiers for the Islamic Revolution. Extensive research has been carried out on this subject.

The shattering of this traditional management and the efforts made over those six years and afterwards to replace it with cooperative management or agricultural joint-stock companies all ended in failure. I saw with my own eyes how, when the revolution took place and the state withdrew its backing from these agricultural joint-stock companies, the peasants themselves looted the offices of those companies and set fire to the tractors—even though they could have put them to optimal use. The point is that traditional manageability exists. One must understand this manageability so that, if we wish to replace it with a new model of management, it is grounded in knowledge of this tradition. According to a brief study I have conducted on the manageability of Iranian society, up until the Constitutional Revolution the traditional managers of the people were, on one side, the khans among the tribes and nomadic clans; on another side, the landlords (*arbabs*); on another side, the turbaned clerics, who had established themselves as social, traditional, and doctrinal leaders; and, to a lesser extent, the bazaar and bazaar merchants—though the bazaar operated more as a network than as a management hierarchy. Guilds also existed, though they were very weak. Even the monarchy and royal administration were virtually tribal: the shah and the court were always the leaders of the tribe that held sway over Iran, such as the Qajars, Safavids, Afsharids, and Zands. Indeed, it was only from the beginning of the Pahlavi era that this arrangement was disrupted. Thus we had a form of manageability that was pyramidal. Four of these—leaving aside the bazaar, which was networked—namely the monarchy, the tribe (khan), the land (*arbab-ra'iyat*), and the clergy (the relationship between the *marja'-e taqlid*, or source of emulation, and the *muqallid*, the devout follower)—were all pyramidal, meaning one-to-many. One person stands at the apex of the pyramid (under the title of landlord, khan, shah, or *marja'-e taqlid*), and the rest of the population extends downwards with virtually no clearly defined intermediate hierarchy. The people play practically no part in governing or administering. A khan might have a *kalantar* (bailiff or headman), an adviser, or a representative, yet because the khan has spoken and issued an order, that bailiff, representative, or steward must carry it out. Perhaps within the clerical system a certain degree of hierarchy is observed, whereby each cleric is addressed with specific titles corresponding to his scholarly rank; yet even there, it is the *marja'-e taqlid* who issues a ruling or a fatwa. We see that everything returns to the apex of the pyramid, which is ultimately the shah. He can even order the taking of a life, with no court of law involved; even when a judge pronounces a death sentence, it is because the shah has granted such permission. The system is wholly pyramidal: several pyramids coexist side by side—the pyramid of the khans, the pyramid of the landlords, the pyramid of the clerics, and the royal pyramid operating above those other three. Moreover, in regions governed along decentralised, fief-like lines, the role of the royal pyramid recedes and local rulers take its place; yet there too, we see the exact same pyramid. Consequently, individuals in a society situated within these four pyramids cannot simultaneously belong to two pyramids of the same kind: a peasant cannot serve two landlords, a believer does not follow two sources of emulation, and the tribe or clan in which a person lives does not have two khans. In the bazaar, admittedly, the situation differs somewhat: an individual can do business with several merchants, and the relationship is one-to-many and networked. Under this pyramidal mode, an individual cannot live in two fiefdoms at once or have two kings. One stands at the top and all others below; the outcome is that the people bear no responsibility—that is, this system entails neither choice nor responsibility. When a *marja'-e taqlid* issues a decree, the individual cannot disobey and has no right of decision. We saw this during the Tobacco Protest: the person has neither the possibility of disobedience nor a share in decision-making. Both are linked—two sides of the same coin. A person who has no possibility of choosing, and for whom choices are made, naturally learns that they bear no responsibility either. In effect, in this pyramidal type of management, the individual possesses neither the power of choice nor the power of decision, gradually carries this disposition up through tradition, and in practice their manageability becomes that of a mere executor, devoid of responsibility. As a result, the individual progressively loses their civic responsibility; and because they do not choose, they also lose the habit of thinking about how to choose—meaning they lose initiative, creativity, and the capacity to open a space and possibility for new production, new work, new speech, and a new life. They become a person in society trapped, on the one hand, within tradition, and on the other, within a form of management in which they are granted neither responsibility nor the right to choose. Now this individual is expected to encounter modern manageability. In modern manageability, the human being is respected; their individuality is respected. Across the various networks in which they live—for instance, even in the apartment block where they reside—they must reach accommodations with others, and usually representatives take decisions on the individual's behalf. These representatives may be trade union representatives, city councillors, provincial representatives, regional representatives, members of the national parliament, or even representatives on boards administering social insurance and pension systems. Those who represent citizens hold their mandate for a limited term, and if they fail to defend what citizens need and have chosen—if the choices the representatives make do not accord with individuals' wishes and needs—they gradually lose their legitimacy as representatives. The respect any representative enjoys stems from being the articulate voice of the citizens. If they act upon their own personal notions and desires, they gradually lose their place. Conversely, if a representative begins at the neighbourhood level and manages over time to maintain their standing, they may even rise to the office of prime minister or president. For us Iranians, the concrete exemplar of this is the late Dr Mossadegh. We see how he begins, ascends the rungs steadily, and gradually earns the people's trust. Even today, after so many years, the three years of that great man's premiership have left their mark as a fundamental model for all people seeking democratic governance. Every time trust was placed in him, he made honourable use of that trust and strengthened his bond with the people. Thus, in reality, we had four types of management in the past, which raised us within four systems in which we had neither responsibility nor the right to choose. Then, gradually from the Constitutional Revolution onwards, we became acquainted with Western management, whose basis and foundation rest upon representation on behalf of the people. According to democracy, wherever power resides, there lies the right to choose. This right of choice comes from the people, through being the people's representative; that is why we see that in times of crisis, when choosing becomes difficult, referendums are proposed. We step up one level and say: on this matter, everyone must decide. Switzerland is commonly regarded as a very advanced form of democracy. The fundamental reason for this is that the possibility of holding referendums there is very high; one might say that referendums are held even in small localities, not merely at the Swiss national level, and they are practically enforceable, so the people of each canton (the semi-autonomous divisions within Switzerland's territorial structure) can take a decision, hold a referendum, and choose a way of life for themselves. Thus this new management is grounded in representation, whose beginning in Iran lies with the Constitutional Revolution. During the Constitutional Revolution, people very quickly grasped this essential point and, in the first Constitution, proposed the establishment of provincial and municipal councils (*anjomanha-ye eyalati va velayati*) so that at the level of a *velayat* (city) and an *eyalat* (region or province), representatives would decide upon local problems. Accordingly, the first provincial and municipal councils were formed; yet the people could not accept the idea of representation—they could not even understand it. Until then, management for them meant either the khan, the landlord, or the cleric; consequently, these councils came to be composed of clerics, landlords, and khans. Gradually, they stood against the National Consultative Assembly, whose aim was to establish democracy along Western lines, and effectively blocked society's movement towards Western civilisation. Very soon, the National Assembly itself was compelled to dissolve those councils. We see that those who drafted the Constitution were unaware of this problem: they did not realise that models of management must bear a proportion to the people's culture of manageability. The point is not that management must always conform passively to the people's existing manageability—change must occur—but one must pay attention to the people's manageability and, on that basis, prepare the people little by little, educate them gradually, and move forward. It is like driving culture in a society: if it is not cultivated gradually, over two or three generations, naturally drivers who are unprepared to respect the driver beside them, behind them, or in front of them will not obey traffic laws. To sum up, if we adopt a perspective from below, starting from manageability, naturally simpler, more useful, and more appropriate models of management will be able to adapt themselves to society.
